p expansion around 106 (step_04) and 1-hop around 80 (step_06) identify concrete closed triples such as {106,236,792}, {80,175,959} and {80,332,1583}. But when those triples are time-localized, their constituent edges are scattered: the 106/236/792 group fires as isolated single events in bins near 43.4k, 44.4k, then nothing until 49.4k\u201354.9k (step_05), and the 80/175/959 group's ~10 events are spread across 43k\u201347k and 52k\u201355k (step_07), so supporting and 'closing' edges are separated by thousands of time units \u2014 far beyond the 800-unit promptness requirement. Confounder checks: the apparent closure is explained by static reachability plus hub popularity, not temporal ordering \u2014 the 80/332/1583 neighbourhood is busy (17\u201325 events per 1000 units, peaking late in the window, step_08), showing that high recent activity and ignore-time connectivity are present while prompt closure is not. Conclusion: no genuine temporal closure wave."}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_afa3f67f\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"closure_wave\",\n  \"entities\": [6, 10, 17, 37, 57, 72, 80, 87, 106, 111, 114, 116],\n  \"window\": [43373, 55373],\n  \"confidence\": 0.72,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"The window holds 2853 events over 2814 distinct edges (repeat ratio 0.0137, step_01) \u2014 essentially a one-shot, non-reinforcing stream. The listed nodes are individually very sparse: 2\u20135 events each, with distinct_partners equal to event count in every case, i.e. no node repeats a partner (step_02). Static triangles do exist (13 incident triangles, roughly one per node, step_03), and 2-hop expansion around 106 (step_04) and 1-hop around 80 (step_06) identify concrete closed triples such as {106,236,792}, {80,175,959} and {80,332,1583}. But when those triples are time-localized, their constituent edges are scattered: the 106/236/792 group fires as isolated single events in bins near 43.4k, 44.4k, then nothing until 49.4k\u201354.9k (step_05), and the 80/175/959 group's ~10 events are spread across 43k\u201347k and 52k\u201355k (step_07), so supporting and 'closing' edges are separated by thousands of time units \u2014 far beyond the 800-unit promptness requirement. Confounder checks: the apparent closure is explained by static reachability plus hub popularity, not temporal ordering \u2014 the 80/332/1583 neighbourhood is busy (17\u201325 events per 1000 units, peaking late in the window, step_08), showing that high recent activity and ignore-time connectivity are present while prompt closure is not. Conclusion: no genuine temporal closure wave.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 22931, "total_output_tokens": 4003, "total_tokens": 26934, "total_latency_ms": 58425, "llm_calls": 5, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}